# Valgrind Suppression File — UltrafastSecp256k1
#
# Purpose: suppress "still reachable" memory from program-lifetime allocations.
#
# The library maintains a ~2.5 MiB precomputed wNAF/comb table for the
# secp256k1 generator G.  This table is allocated once (first use) and
# intentionally never freed — it lives for the entire process lifetime.
# Valgrind correctly classifies it as "still reachable" (a pointer to
# the block exists at program exit), NOT as a real leak.
#
# What IS still checked (will fail CI if found):
#   • definitely lost   (real leaks)
#   • indirectly lost    (real leaks)
#   • possibly lost      (potential leaks)
#   • invalid read/write (memory errors)
#   • use of uninitialised values
#
# CTest's  -T MemCheck  parser counts "still reachable" as a defect,
# so we suppress it here rather than weakening the error checks.

{
   secp256k1_precomputed_tables_still_reachable
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   obj:*
   ...
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Persistent batch-verify worker pool: thread TLS alive at exit, not a leak
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# detail::batch_worker_pool() (src/cpu/src/batch_verify.cpp) is a deliberately
# leaked singleton. Its destructor would join the workers at static-destruction
# time, which on Windows runs during DLL unload with the loader lock held —
# joining there deadlocks, because the workers need that same lock to exit. The
# code says so at the definition. So the pool's threads are still RUNNING when
# the process exits.
#
# glibc allocates each thread's dynamic thread vector inside pthread_create
# (_dl_allocate_tls -> allocate_dtv -> calloc). For a thread that never exits,
# Valgrind sees that block with no pointer it can follow and reports
# "possibly lost". Nothing is lost: the threads are alive by design and the OS
# reclaims their stacks and TLS at exit.
#
# Without this, the gate fails intermittently — it depends on how many workers
# the pool actually spawned before the process ended:
#
#   960 bytes in 3 blocks are possibly lost in loss record 5 of 7
#     at calloc / allocate_dtv (dl-tls.c:370) / _dl_allocate_tls (dl-tls.c:629)
#     by allocate_stack / pthread_create@@GLIBC_2.34
#     by std::thread::_M_start_thread
#     by batch_pool.hpp:40 <- batch_verify.cpp:76
#     by secp256k1::ecdsa_batch_verify_mt (batch_verify.cpp:787)
#
# Scoped to the pthread_create TLS allocation specifically. Real leaks in the
# batch path — anything allocated by our own code and dropped — are still
# reported, and definite/indirect leaks anywhere are untouched.
{
   secp256k1_batch_worker_pool_thread_tls_possibly_lost
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:_dl_allocate_tls
   ...
   fun:pthread_create*
   ...
}
